.TH EVENT_LOOP_REMOVE 3 "2026-07-12" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
event_loop_remove \- deregister a selectable from an event loop
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_retval_t event_loop_remove(event_loop loop, event_reg *reg);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Deregisters reg.  Safe to call from within a callback for the very
registration being removed (self-removal on error is a common pattern),
as well as from any other thread, including concurrently with an
in-flight dispatch for the same registration; teardown is deferred until
any in-progress callback returns.  No further callback for this
registration is ever invoked after this call returns, even one already
collected (e.g. sitting queued for a dispatch worker thread with
num_reactor_threads > 1) but not yet actually started; callers may free
whatever the registration's own arg points to immediately after this
call returns without racing a stale callback invocation.

Does not close an fd or affect a queue's own lifetime; only the
event_loop's registration bookkeeping is released, mirroring
selectable_from_fd's existing "caller owns the fd" contract.

Idempotent: calling event_loop_remove() a second time on an
already-removed reg returns ccol_success without side effects.  The reg
handle itself remains safe to pass to a subsequent event_loop_modify()
or event_loop_remove() call after removal (both are documented to
detect this and return gracefully), but must not be otherwise
dereferenced by the caller once removed.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
loop  event_loop the registration belongs to.
reg   Registration to remove.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success        Removed (or was already removed).
ccol_invalid_args   loop or reg is NULL.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
Safe to call from any thread, including concurrently with dispatch for
the same registration and from within that registration's own callback.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
void on_readable(event_loop loop, ccol_selectable *sel, void *arg) {
    conn_ctx_t *ctx = (conn_ctx_t *)arg;
    char buf[512];
    ssize_t n = read(sel->fd, buf, sizeof(buf));
    if (n <= 0) {
        event_loop_remove(loop, ctx->reg);  /* self-removal, safe */
        close(sel->fd);
        free(ctx);
        return;
    }
    /* ... */
}
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR event_loop_add (3),
.BR event_loop_modify (3),
.BR event_loop_pause (3),
.BR event_loop_resume (3)
